Week02 Reflection
Software Process Models

Brief A
For the Aegis flight-control software I would choose the Waterfall model.
The main reason for this is that the requirements are already very well understood and are not expected to change. 
The software is also being used in an aircraft, meaning that a software failure could potentially put people's lives at risk.
Because of this the software needs to go through a very strict testing and certification process.
Waterfall would work well because each stage can be documented and checked before moving onto the next stage.
The project also requires complete traceability from the requirements to the code and then to the testing evidence. 
This makes Waterfall a good fit because it is a very structured process.

Brief B
For the Riverside Bakery website I would choose RAD. 
The owner does not know exactly what she wants the website to look like and is likely to change her mind after seeing a working version. 
RAD would allow the developers to quickly create versions of the website and get feedback from her. 
The three week deadline also makes a fast development process useful.

Brief C
For the CampusCircle app I would choose the Incremental model. 
The founders expect the requirements to change after real students start using the app. 
The team could release a basic version first and then add features such as RSVP and chat based on feedback.

The main thing I have learned from these examples is that there is no single process model that works for every software project. 
The model needs to be chosen based on the requirements, risk, deadline and how involved the customer needs to be.
